This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

**Version Used**:

.NET SDK 11.0.100, compiler `5.12.0-1.26529.118 (297acf930fb0f5c07fd4e51d78e521b4ad2e5d1f)`, language version 15.0 (`latest`).

**Steps to Reproduce**:

1. Declare an `Add` method that takes an `IEnumerable<TElement>` inside an `extension` block for `ICollection<T>`.
2. Use it from a collection initializer, passing an argument whose static type is `IEnumerable<T>`, such as a LINQ result or a local typed as `IEnumerable<T>`.
3. Compile with `<Nullable>enable</Nullable>`.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

var source = new List<string>();
IEnumerable<string> seq = source;

_ = new Holder { List = { seq } };                           // CS8620 (wrong)
_ = new Holder { List = { source.DefaultIfEmpty("x") } };    // CS8620 (wrong)
_ = new Holder { List = { source } };                        // OK: List<string> argument happens to convert to ICollection<string>
new List<string>().Add(seq);                                 // OK: direct call outside an initializer

class Holder
{
    public List<string> List { get; } = [];
}

static class Ext
{
    extension<T>(ICollection<T> collection)
    {
        public void Add<TElement>(IEnumerable<TElement> elements) where TElement : T
        {
            foreach (var e in elements) collection.Add(e);
        }
    }
}
```

When `Add` is rewritten as a classic extension method (`public static void Add<T, TElement>(this ICollection<T> collection, IEnumerable<TElement> elements)`), the same code compiles with no warnings.

**Diagnostic Id**:

`CS8620: Argument of type 'IEnumerable<string>' cannot be used for parameter 'collection' of type 'ICollection<string>' in 'Ext.extension<string>(ICollection<string>)' due to differences in the nullability of reference types.`

**Expected Behavior**:

No warnings. Every type in the repro is non-nullable `string`, and calling the same method directly (`list.Add(seq)`) or as a classic extension method gives no diagnostic.

**Actual Behavior**:

CS8620 is reported for each collection-initializer element whose type is `IEnumerable<T>`. The message names the extension block's **receiver** parameter `collection`, not the method parameter `elements`. So when nullable analysis handles an extension-block `Add` called from a collection initializer, it seems to check the element argument against the receiver parameter, an off-by-one in how arguments are paired with parameters. When the argument also happens to convert to the receiver type (a `List<T>` argument converts to `ICollection<T>`), no warning appears, which hides the bug in many cases. With `TreatWarningsAsErrors` the build fails.

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        "Hello".MyExt(); // No warning (unexpected)

        Extensions.MyExt("Hello"); // Warning (expected)
    }
}

[Obsolete("Don't use")]
public static class Extensions
{
    public static void MyExt(this string s) { }
}
```

